<article class="env-lemma" id="08H2">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08H2">Lemma <span data-tag="08H2">7.14.10</span>.</a> Let $f : \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{C}$ be a morphism of sites given by the functor $u : \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}$. Given any object $V$ of $\mathcal{D}$ there exists a covering $\{ V_ j \to V\} $ such that for every $j$ there exists a morphism $V_ j \to u(U_ j)$ for some object $U_ j$ of $\mathcal{C}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $f^{-1} = u_ s$ is exact we have $f^{-1}* = *$ where $*$ denotes the final object of the category of sheaves (Example <a href="/tag/00W3" data-tag="00W3">7.10.2</a>). Since $f^{-1}* = u_ s*$ is the sheafification of $u_ p*$ we see there exists a covering $\{ V_ j \to V\} $ such that $(u_ p*)(V_ j)$ is nonempty. Since $(u_ p*)(V_ j)$ is a colimit over the category $\mathcal{I}^ u_{V_ j}$ whose objects are morphisms $V_ j \to u(U)$ the lemma follows. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>